---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import { modelRange, VENDOR_RANGE_AS_OF, vendorDivergences } from '../../lib/firmware';
import { BITFIG } from '../../lib/entropy-models';

// Checked against the firmware release histories, release commits and signed
// signed-release record. Search-space figures remain attributed estimates.
// Version boundaries interpolate from src/lib/firmware.ts, the same module the
// risk overview table renders from, so the two cannot drift apart.
const mk2 = modelRange('Mk2');
const mk3 = modelRange('Mk3');
const mk4 = modelRange('Mk4');
const mk5 = modelRange('Mk5');
const q = modelRange('Q');

// Where the vendor publishes a narrower range than this site classifies, the
// same module carries the divergence, so the model table, the release-boundary
// table and the risk overview all state one set of boundaries.
const divergence = vendorDivergences().find((d) => d.models.includes('Mk3')) ?? null;
const vendorRangeCell = divergence
  ? `<small><a href="#vendor-lower-bound">Coinkite states <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code> to <code>${divergence.affectedTo}</code></a></small>`
  : '';
const vendorStartMark = divergence
  ? `<small><a href="#vendor-lower-bound">Coinkite starts at <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code></a></small>`
  : '';
const vendorRangeSentence = divergence
  ? ` Coinkite's advisory starts this range at <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code>, ` +
    `while <a href="#vendor-lower-bound">this site includes <code>${divergence.affectedFrom}</code></a> ` +
    `on the release-commit and signed-release evidence, which does not itself establish ` +
    `how widely that build was installed before <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code> ` +
    `arrived twelve days later.`
  : '';
// The one-sentence summary of the chain-analysis corroboration travels with the
// range in src/lib/firmware.ts, so the risk overview footnote and this callout
// cannot end up stating it differently. What belongs here rather than in the
// module is the verbatim wording. The three limits that bound it are a rung of
// their own below, so the callout states the corroboration once.
const corroborationBlock = divergence?.corroboration
  ? `
<br /><br />
One piece of outside evidence bears on the same boundary from a different direction. ${divergence.corroboration} Galaxy's wording is "The vulnerable Coldcard firmware shipped on March 17, 2021 around block 674,951. Not one of the coins we have identified in Waves 1-3 taken was created before that block." <a href="#galaxy-boundary-limits">Three limits belong with that.</a>`
  : '';
const body = `<div class="table-wrap">
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="data-table sortable">
<caption>Firmware generation status by model and release range</caption>
<thead><tr>
  <th scope="col" class="nw">Model</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Firmware at generation</th>
  <th scope="col" class="nw">Status</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Candidate-space note</th><th scope="col" class="wide">Notes</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-safe">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk1</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>&le; v3.0.6</code><small>final Mk1 build, 19 Dec 2019</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--ok">Not affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits</td>
  <td class="wide">Predates the libngu migration.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-safe">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2 / Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>&le; ${mk3.lastSafe}</code><small>up to 14 Jan 2021</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--ok">Not affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits</td>
  <td class="wide">Seed generation used <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code>, which reaches the STM32 TRNG directly. The next published release, v4.0.0, is the first one containing the affected path.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk2.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk2.affectedTo}</code><small>17 Mar 2021 to 26 Jun 2023</small>${vendorRangeCell}</td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="40">Block: <code>2^0</code> fixed state;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk23LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
  <td class="wide">Generation moved to <code>ngu.random</code>, and this hardware has no secure-element reseed at all. Mk2 never received a v5.x release. Coinkite's advisory names this model directly, stating that the issue "is present on Mk2 and Mk3 firmware versions 4.0.1 through 4.1.9 inclusive"; that wording dates from its revision of 1 August 2026 at 18:35 UTC, before which the advisory named only the Mk3.${vendorRangeSentence} <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk3.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk3.affectedTo}</code><small>17 Mar 2021 to 26 Jun 2023</small>${vendorRangeCell}</td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="40">Block: <code>2^0</code> fixed state;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk23LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
  <td class="wide"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite described the entropy as approximately 40 bits</a>; LLFOURN published a separate <code>~${BITFIG.llfournMk3}</code> model. Firmware v4.1.9 remained the last published Mk3 release until v4.2.0 was published on 31 July 2026.${vendorRangeSentence} <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">The model register states each assumption.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk4</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk4.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk4.affectedTo}</code><small>14 Mar 2022 to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
  <td class="wide">On the normal successful boot path, <code>rng_seeding()</code> derives a value from secure-element output but carries at most 32 bits into libngu. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Coinkite, LLFOURN and Talip publish different broader models.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk5</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk5.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk5.affectedTo}</code><small>5 Mar to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
  <td class="wide">The first repository release explicitly supporting Mk5 is v5.5.0. Both published pre-hotfix Mk5 releases are affected. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Q</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${q.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${q.affectedTo}</code><small>8 Feb 2024 to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
  <td class="wide">All Q builds recorded in the published release history before v1.5.0Q are affected, including the v1.0.0Q production release of 10 Mar 2024. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2 / Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk3.fix}</code><small>official Mk3 X update: 31 Jul 2026, 13:43 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
  <td class="wide">The release history describes a hotfix that corrects the entropy bug and allows new seed generation. Coinkite's advisory lists the fixed release as "Mk2/Mk3: version 4.2.0 or later" and describes "Fixed Mk2/Mk3 firmware version 4.2.0" as released. Before the advisory's revision of 1 August 2026 at 18:35 UTC it named only the Mk3, and the vendor downloads-page listing of 4.2.0 for "COLDCARD Mk3 and Mk2" was the only vendor evidence covering the Mk2. The source contains the hardware-RNG fix; this archive has not reproduced the vendor build, installed it on either model or independently inspected the signed binary.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk4 / Mk5</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk4.fix}</code><small>signed release: 31 Jul 2026, 05:18 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
  <td class="wide">The signed-release record precedes source release commit <code>3238f6f</code> at 05:19 UTC. That commit contains the source-level fix. The advisory separately names Edge <code>v6.6.0X</code>; neither published binary has been independently checked here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Q</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${q.fix}</code><small>signed release: 31 Jul 2026, 05:17 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
  <td class="wide">The signed-release record precedes source release commit <code>3238f6f</code> at 05:19 UTC. That commit contains the source-level fix. The advisory separately names Edge <code>v6.6.0QX</code>; neither published binary has been independently checked here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>OPENDIME</strong></td><td class="nw">V3.0 r.A<small>firmware 2.2.0</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">One-device test reported</span></td><td class="nw">user entropy incorporation reported for one unit</td>
  <td class="wide"><a href="/record/sources/zherbert-opendime-entropy-test/">Foundation Devices co-founder and CEO Zach Herbert reports reconstructing the final private key from the exact user-provided test vector, USB serial and revealed nonce.</a> Foundation sells competing hardware-wallet products. The test covers one unit, does not assess internal-nonce quality and was not independently reproduced here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>TAPSIGNER</strong></td><td class="nw">All</td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Vendor-reported</span></td><td class="nw">not independently assessed here</td>
  <td class="wide"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite reports that TAPSIGNER is not affected because it uses a different codebase.</a> <a href="/record/sources/robhamilton-opendime-tapsigner-scope/">Rob Hamilton notes that its closed source prevents first-hand verification</a> and reports only preliminary evidence that it does not use the affected libngu path.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>SATSCARD</strong></td><td class="nw">All</td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Vendor-reported</span></td><td class="nw">not independently assessed here</td>
  <td class="wide"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite reports that SATSCARD is not affected because it uses a different codebase.</a> This archive has not independently reviewed that codebase.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>

<p class="cap">For the named 31 July hotfixes, the source-level change is verified. The signed release files are vendor-published, but this archive has not reproduced the builds or independently inspected the binaries.</p>

<h2>Release boundary record</h2>

<p>Purchase date does not determine exposure; the published build in use at generation does. The selected releases below identify the relevant boundaries from the per-model history files.</p>

